ifications: RoHS (db), UL, cUL, ENEC, VDE, CE, CCC, REACH, TAA (from product pages) -> /"], "company_type": "Manufacturer", "makes_products": "yes", "type_evidence": "CCX is proud to be a best in class, North American manufacturer of custom and high speed cables and connectivity solutions. We produce custom cables according to customer specifications as well as high speed fiber, copper and power cables and a complete suite of optics.", "founded_year": "", "employee_count": "", "cage_code": "", "min_order": "", "export_compliance": ["TAA compliant"], "languages": [], "social_links": [], "ownership_tags": []}'::jsonb, updated_at=now() WHERE slug='ccx-corporation' AND approved AND profile IS NULL;
UPDATE companies SET description='The Center for Composite Materials (CCM) was established in 1974 at the University of Delaware in Newark, Delaware. As a university-based research center, it operates under the College of Engineering and draws faculty from multiple departments including mechanical engineering, materials science, and chemical engineering. The center was created to advance interdisciplinary research in composite materials and has since been designated a Center of Excellence eight times by the National Science Foundation and the Department of Defense.

CCM provides a full spectrum of composites research and development services, from material characterization and mechanical testing to process development, prototyping, and component validation. Its 53,688-square-foot facility houses equipment valued at more than $25 million, including autoclaves, resin infusion systems, automated fiber placement, additive manufacturing platforms, and a suite of thermal, mechanical, and nondestructive evaluation instruments. In a recent year, more than 51 faculty members, 41 research professionals and postdoctoral fellows, and 71 graduate and undergraduate students participated in over 50 research projects with annual expenditures exceeding $17 million.

The center serves the automotive, aerospace, defense, and energy sectors. It maintains long-standing collaborations with government agencies such as the Department of Energy, NASA, DARPA, and the U.S. Army’s TARDEC, as well as with industry partners including BAE Systems, major OEMs, and Tier‑1 suppliers. More than 350 companies, ranging from startups to Fortune 500 firms, have engaged with CCM through its University‑Industry Consortium.

CCM distinguishes itself through end-to-end integration of materials science, process engineering, and validation under one roof, enabling rapid translation of laboratory discoveries into manufacturable solutions. Its mission emphasizes technology transfer, workforce development, and the creation of predictive modeling tools that link atomistic physics to composite performance.', certifications=ARRAY[]::text[], profile='{"capabilities": ["High Pressure-Resin Transfer Molding (HP-RTM)", "Liquid Injection Molding (LIMS)", "Autoclave processing", "Out-of-autoclave processing", "Pultrusion", "Automated fiber placement", "Resin infusion", "Additive manufacturing (polymer and composite)", "Ultrasonic additive manufacturing", "Mechanical testing (tensile, compression, flexural, fatigue, impact)", "High-rate loading (Hopkinson-Kolsky bar)", "Digital Image Correlation (2D/3D)", "Thermal analysis (DSC, TGA, TMA, DMA, DVS)", "Rheometry and gas pycnometry", "Microscopy and imaging (SEM, TEM, AFM, NanoIR, confocal)", "Non-destructive evaluation (ultrasonic, X-ray CT, thermography)", "Surface and interface testing (adhesion, wettability, fiber pull-out)", "Molecular dynamics and ab initio simulation", "Continuum-level FEA and process modeling", "AI-assisted predictive modeling", "Digital twin and virtual prototyping", "Rapid prototyping and iterative testing", "Tooling and prototype fabrication", "Standards and certification support (aerospace, automotive)"], "industries": ["Aviation & Aerospace", "Automotive", "Defense", "Energy Science & Engineering"], "materials": ["Polymers", "Metals", "Ceramics", "Composite materials", "High-temperature polymers"], "equipment": ["Autoclaves", "Ovens and presses", "Resin infusion systems", "Automated fiber placement equipment", "Additive manufacturing systems (polymer and composite)", "Ultrasonic additive manufacturing system", "Hopkinson-Kolsky bar (high-rate loading)", "X-ray computed tomography (CT) scanner", "Scanning electron microscope 